For international [NodeSchool](http://nodeschool.io) day, here's how to create a workshop using [adventure-runner](http://npm.im/adventure-runner).

## Setup

npm install --save adventure-runner

## Structure

- `runner.js` - The brain of the workshop
- `problems/` - Where all problems are located
- `package.json` - Like any other module

## runner.js

```javascript
var runner = require('adventure-runner');
runner('math-adventure', './problems');

## Problem Definition
javascript
// problems/addition/index.js
exports.problem = 'Write a program that adds two numbers...';

exports.verify = function(args, cb) {
  var proposed = require(process.cwd() + '/' + args[0]);
  if (proposed(3, 4) == 7) {
    console.log("You have solved it!");
    return cb(true);
  }
};
```

A fun CLI hack to automatically tweet about every git commit.

## Prerequisites

npm install ntwitter
npm install -g grunt-cli

## Custom Grunt Tweet Task

```javascript
grunt.registerTask('tweet', 'Tweet the commit info', function() {
  var twitter = require('twitter');
  var shell = require('shelljs');

  var commit_comment = shell.exec('git log -1 --pretty=%B').output
    .replace(/(\r\n|\n|\r)/gm, '');
  var repo = shell.exec('basename $(git remote show -n origin | grep Fetch | cut -d: -f2-)').output
    .replace(/(\r\n|\n|\r)/gm, '');
  var author = shell.exec("git --no-pager show -s --format='%an'").output
    .replace(/(\r\n|\n|\r)/gm, '');

  var tweet = util.format('%s on %s by %s', commit_comment, repo, author);

  var twit = new twitter({
    consumer_key: 'Twitter',
    consumer_secret: 'API',
    access_token_key: 'keys',
    access_token_secret: 'go here'
  });

  twit.updateStatus(tweet, function(err, data) {
    console.log(data);
  });
});
```

Have a pre-commit hook at `.git/hooks/pre-commit` calling `grunt tweet` to tweet on every commit!

The tweet would be something like: `'jshint suggestions from @passy on yo.git by Hemanth.HM'`

Yes, Plugin-free access to camera/microphone with HTML5!

Welcome to the post-flash revolution aka smartphone development. Flash isn't even available on Android 4.1.

AFAIK Ericsson labs were first to experiment on handling devices with HTML5, now with the `navigator.getUserMedia` we can handle both camera and microphone devices with ease.

It's important to note that this works with Chrome 18.0.1009.0 with the **--enable-media-stream** flag set, Firefox nightly and Opera 12.

In Chrome type `chrome://flags` in your address bar. Find 'Enable MediaStream' and enable it.

**Simple code to capture voice and video:**

```javascript
navigator.getUserMedia = navigator.webkitGetUserMedia || navigator.getUserMedia;

window.URL = window.URL || window.webkitURL;

navigator.getUserMedia({audio: true, video: true}, function(stream) {
  var video = document.querySelector('video');
  video.src = window.URL.createObjectURL(stream);
}, function(e) {
  console.log(e);
});
```

